From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thelamer <ryankuba@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 18:22:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Code the blocks the caller marks constant as skips
 without analysis

A screen encoder knows which 16x16 blocks of a picture are the frame before's; the analysis of
a coding unit of such blocks still derives and costs merge candidates, searches motion, tries
intra modes, and recurses into the split. x265_picture.constantBlocks carries that map, one
byte a block, and a P-slice coding unit whose every block is marked is coded as a 2Nx2N skip
from the zero-motion merge candidate of the first reference, with the motion compensation, the
skip's rate-distortion cost, and the delta-QP bookkeeping the full path gives a skip, and
nothing else; where no merge candidate is that vector the full path decides as before. A block
left unmarked is analysed as before, so the caller marks only blocks whose reference already
holds them at the quality wanted.

A still 1920x1080 screen, every block marked, took 8.4 ms a frame on one thread where it took
15.1 and 4.5 on eight where it took 5.2; a 256x256 change beside marked blocks 27.8 against
32.5 and 12.4 against 13.0; at 2560x1600 a still frame 15.7 against 29.6 and 8.8 against 9.4, a
change 36.0 against 45.4 and 16.8 against 17.6. A frame with nothing marked codes as before, bit
for bit.
